## Package comparison

The Phase 3 package remains aligned with the approved plan, and the implementation stays within the allowed server, executable, and focused-test files. This round resolves several prior gaps, but the package's mandatory ownership matrix is not yet complete.

## Findings

### High — Blocked work and acceptance-vs-close remain outside the evidence

The coder result explicitly leaves blocked-command shutdown, blocked-sampling shutdown, and acceptance racing close as residual risks. The suite therefore still does not prove that closing a connection interrupts a waiting command with no response after provider release, that server closure waits for blocked sampling and exact provider/listener/socket finalization, or that every socket accepted during shutdown is enrolled-and-finalized or immediately destroyed. These are central Phase 3 acceptance gates rather than later protocol or load work.

### High — Signal ownership has only completion-path evidence

The new injectable `waitForSignal` seam and signal-completion test are appropriate, but the package requires handler removal on both completion and interruption. No test interrupts the waiting Effect and proves both handlers are removed. There is also still no runtime executable-path evidence that signal-driven Layer cleanup completes and that a cleanup failure produces nonzero process status while retaining tagged diagnostics.

### High — Exact graph and command-admission ownership remain incomplete

The two-client test now proves one listener, one provider source subscription, immediate status/state, and a subsequent snapshot at both clients. It still does not instrument one coordinator or exact connection/graph finalization. The command test invokes two client Promises concurrently and asserts provider call order, but has no command-admission signal establishing the sockets' observed FIFO enrollment order. The package explicitly requires exact graph ownership and commands from both clients entering the single coordinator FIFO in observed order.

### Medium — Server-shutdown late-write evidence does not prove forwarding finalization

The write-attempt counter is an improvement, but the test waits for only the first socket data event, closes the entire graph, and emits through the legacy provider after its subscription has already been disposed. The unchanged write count can therefore result from provider-source disposal alone. Instrument and await both forwarding-fiber finalizers before emitting the late event, or otherwise inject directly at the forwarding source, so the test proves connection-child finalization and not merely outer provider teardown.

### Medium — The socket-defect test proves observation but only weakly proves healthy-peer behavior

The injected server-side socket error now reaches the synchronous hook and leaves an already-connected peer undestroyed. The test does not exercise replay, updates, or commands on that peer after the defect, nor reconnect through the listener, so it does not yet establish the package's requirement that the healthy peer and listener continue operating.

## Verification

The coder reports 15 server tests, 65 coordinator/provider tests, all package targets, and all static scans passing. Those checks do not supply the missing deterministic ownership evidence above.
